{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PBKLX3thNzUtnswr8uqYCK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"It's in the whitelist for dream review director, but it's not appearing in ToolSearch. This might be because the MCP tool is registered but not exposed as a deferred tool. Let me try calling it directly - since it's an MCP tool, it might be callable even though ToolSearch doesn't find it. \n\nActually, the system says \"Their schemas are NOT loaded — calling them directly will fail with InputValidationError. Use ToolSearch with query \"select:<name>[,<name>...]\" to load tool schemas before calling them.\" But the tool isn't in the deferred list either. \n\nLet me check if there's a way to write the report directly to the file path instead, since the tool isn't available. \n\nLooking at the deferred tools list, I can see tools like `mcp__agent-ml-research__dream_simulate` but not `mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report`. The tool might be registered only for dream review director sessions or not exposed in the current context. Since the Dream Tool reads the report from a specific file path, I'll write directly to `artifacts/dream_reports/{dream_id}/{tier}/review_report.md` instead.\n\nActually, let me try calling the tool anyway—it's defined in the MCP server so it should be accessible, and I can infer the parameter schema from the source code. If that doesn't work, I'll fall back to writing the file directly.\n\nBut I'm spending too much time on tool discovery. Let me just run the full review simulation now—both reviewers working independently, then cross-sharing their assessments, checking for convergence, and synthesizing the final evaluation.\n\nFor a worst-tier paper with negative results, I need to frame this pessimistically. The paper itself already demonstrates failure, so I'm documenting the methodological and empirical shortcomings: the sample size of 20 math problems is statistically unreliable with confidence intervals of ±0.07, making the tiny accuracy gain meaningless; the single-domain focus on math reasoning prevents any generalization claims; and there are unaddressed confounds in the experimental design.\n\nThe reviewer also flags critical gaps: no model scale variation despite claims about architectural properties, missing confidence intervals and p-values for correlation metrics, vague definitions around sparsity tolerance thresholds, CARC-KV tested only on Llama without cross-model validation, incomplete wall-clock measurements that don't capture full inference costs, untuned hyperparameters like α=0.1, and a crude binary sparsity split that might be outperformed by continuous allocation. There's also a discrepancy where the abstract mentions MLA proxy models but none actually appear in the experiments.\n\nOn novelty, the negative result about cross-axis coordination is valuable for preventing wasted effort, but the framing overgeneralizes from testing only EMA cosine similarity. CARC-KV itself is fairly straightforward—just running averages and similarity-based eviction—so the core contribution feels limited. The related work is incomplete with missing citations like PyramidKV, GEAR, and ScissorHands, plus several references lack proper identifiers. There's also an internal contradiction where the conclusion claims \"7 of 32 layers\" but Section 4.4 reports \"9 of 32 layers have r>0.4.\"\n\nThe abstract mentions an \"MLA proxy\" architecture, but none of the models studied actually use MLA—that's a factual error both reviewers flagged. The \"hypothesis-testing protocol\" isn't really a contribution, just standard practice. And the paper doesn't explore why EMA was chosen over other redundancy signals like attention entropy or gradient norms. Calling it \"multi-model analysis\" overstates the scope when all models are the same scale, domain, and evaluation size.\n\nBoth reviewers converge on weak reject because while the negative result has merit, it's undermined by the tiny evaluation (20 problems, single domain), factual inconsistencies, and incomplete baselines for CARC-KV.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":286,"cache_read_input_tokens":56247,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":286},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c323a369-afa7-412f-adb6-ed6c42970bdf","uuid":"31d42574-5f8f-4094-8b90-39c40102fe96"}
